      "topic": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch10/10.2.2",
      "title": "10.2.2 Finding oxidation number of an element in a compound or a radical",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 215,
          "pdf_to": 216,
          "note": "Topic 10.2.2 with Sample Problems 10.1 to 10.3 and Quick Check 10.1, printed pages 215-216."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The oxidation number or state of any atom of an element present in a compound or a radical can be determined by making use of the above-mentioned rules."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Compounds of a metal with a non-metal"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A metal always has the positive Ox. No. and nonmetals have the negative Ox. state For example, in sodium sulfide, Na₂S:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "2(+1) + \\text{S} = 0",
            "text_alt": "2(+1) + S = 0"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "+2 + \\text{S} = 0",
            "text_alt": "+2 + S = 0"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{S} = -2",
            "text_alt": "S = -2"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Compounds of a non-metal with a non-metal"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In compounds containing two different non-metals, the sign of the Ox. No. depends on the electronegativity of each atom. The most electronegative element is given the negative sign."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Sample Problem 10.1"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Find the oxidation No. of S in Sulfur dioxide, SO₂."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Ox. No. of each O atom = –2"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For two oxygen atoms = 2 × (–2) = –4"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "SO₂ has no charge, so the total Ox. No. is zero. So,"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "S + 2(-2) = 0"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "S - 4 = 0"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "S = +4"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Ox. No. of S = +4"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
